.TH CYAML_NODE_SET_TAG 3 "2026-08-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_node_set_tag \- attach, replace, or clear a node's YAML tag
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

ccol_retval_t cyaml_node_set_tag(cyaml node, const char *tag);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Attaches a tag to node, replaces node's tag, or clears it.  The node
makes its own copy of tag.

Only the metadata changes.  The function never changes the type or
the value that node has.

A core-schema tag names exactly one node type:

  CYAML_TAG_NULL   -> CYAML_NULL
  CYAML_TAG_BOOL   -> CYAML_BOOL
  CYAML_TAG_INT    -> CYAML_INTEGER
  CYAML_TAG_FLOAT  -> CYAML_FLOAT
  CYAML_TAG_STR    -> CYAML_STRING
  CYAML_TAG_SEQ    -> CYAML_LIST
  CYAML_TAG_MAP    -> CYAML_DICTIONARY

A core-schema tag on a node of any other type is refused with
ccol_invalid_args and the node is left untouched, because
cyaml_parse(3) would refuse such a pairing or read it back as a
different type.  If you want a string node, you have to build one
with cyaml_create_string(3) and then tag it.  A custom tag, meaning
any tag other than those seven, is valid on a node of any type.

A custom tag set here survives a later cyaml_set(3) call on the same
node.  A core-schema tag survives such a call only as long as it
names the node's type, since cyaml_set(3) drops a core-schema tag
that the new value doesn't match.  cyaml_serialize(3) and
cyaml_serialize_flow(3) write a custom tag in its fully resolved
verbatim form and leave a core-schema tag out, because implicit
typing gives its node the right type without it.

A tag must be well-formed UTF-8.  One that is not is refused with
ccol_invalid_args, and node keeps the tag it has.  Any other
non-empty string is accepted as a tag, even one with a character
that the URI characters of YAML 1.2 don't allow literally in the
serialized output, such as a \&'>', a line break, a '%', a space, a
control character or any non-ASCII character.  The serializer writes
each byte of such a character as a percent escape, so parsing the
tag again gives exactly the string you set here.

The empty string is not a valid tag.  It is refused with
ccol_invalid_args, and node keeps the tag it has.  The
serializer writes a custom tag in its verbatim "!<...>" form and
cyaml_parse(3) doesn't accept "!<>", so an empty tag would give a
document that this library cannot parse again.  Pass NULL to clear a
tag.  "!" alone is the non-specific tag, which no node carries, so it
is refused with ccol_invalid_args too.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  The target node, of any type.  For NULL the function reports
      ccol_invalid_args and changes nothing.
tag   The new tag string, which the node copies.  It must be
      well-formed UTF-8 and must not be the empty string.  NULL
      clears the node's tag.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.  ccol_invalid_args when node is NULL, when
tag is the empty string or "!", when tag is not well-formed UTF-8,
or when tag is a core-schema tag that doesn't name node's type, and
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.  On every failure
node keeps the tag it has.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_node_tag (3),
.BR cyaml_set (3),
.BR cyaml_create_string (3)
